{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"**Authorized participants (APs)** are firms—typically large broker-dealers—that can create or redeem ETF shares directly with the fund, usually in large blocks called creation units. They exchange a basket of securities (or cash) for ETF shares, or vice versa, helping keep the ETF’s share supply aligned with demand and its market price near net asset value.\n\n**Market makers** quote bids and offers for ETF shares on an exchange, providing liquidity to investors who buy or sell. They may hold inventory or hedge their exposure with the ETF’s underlying securities, futures, or other instruments.\n\nA firm can be both an AP and a market maker, but the roles differ: APs transact with the fund to create or redeem shares; market makers facilitate secondary-market trading between investors. AP activity can support price alignment, but neither role guarantees tight spreads or eliminates premiums and discounts."}}
